    "id": "idols",
    "number": 2,
    "title": "Idols",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Here's something we don't like to admit: so many things in our lives can become a god to us. Alcohol. Drugs. Our wives. Our husbands. Our kids. Going to the gym. Notice that the list doesn't sort neatly into bad things and good things. Some of these things are not evil in and of themselves. Some of them are gifts. But the moment we turn to them instead of Jesus Christ, we are worshiping an idol.",
      "Think about how this works in a marriage. A wife can become an idol. A husband can become an idol. It doesn't look like worship. It looks like love. But somewhere along the way, one person becomes the one who is supposed to make us feel whole, the one who is supposed to fill the empty place inside us. And no husband and no wife can carry that. They were never built to. When we ask a spouse to be our source of worth, our security, and our peace, we crush them under a weight that belongs to God alone. And then when they fail us, and they will, we feel like the floor has dropped out from under us.",
      "Now think about being a parent. Kids can become an idol, and this one is especially hard to see, because we're praised for it. We call it sacrifice. We call it devotion. And a lot of it is good, because God gave us these children to love. But watch what happens when a child becomes the center of everything. Our mood rises and falls with their report card, their ball game, their choices. Our identity gets tied up in who they turn out to be. Psychologists have a word for this when the boundaries between parent and child collapse: enmeshment.",
      "Research on it describes parents whose own identity becomes dependent on a child's achievements, and it describes the cost on both sides. The child struggles to develop a sense of self, and the parent often watches their relationships outside the family deteriorate because all their focus goes to the children. A good gift, turned into a god, ends up hurting the very person we're trying to protect.",
      "Most idols aren't bad things. That's what makes them so hard to spot. Sometimes they are good things that we slowly lift into the place that belongs to God. And here's a test worth sitting with: whatever is so central to your life that losing it would leave you feeling like life isn't worth living has become more than a gift. Take a minute with that. If you lost your marriage, your children's approval, your health, your business, would you still have a reason to get out of bed? If the honest answer is no, something has taken a seat that was meant for God.",
      "This is not a new problem. God spoke to Israel through the prophet Ezekiel: \"Son of man, these men have taken their idols into their hearts, and set the stumbling block of their iniquity before their faces.\" These weren't idols on a shelf. They were idols in the heart.",
      "Paul says the very same thing to the church in Colossae. He names covetousness, simply wanting something too much, and he calls it exactly what it is: \"which is idolatry.\"",
      "So this isn't about carved images anymore. It's about anything, anything, that takes God's seat in your heart. And once you see that, you start to see idolatry everywhere. Paul doesn't soften what's at stake. In Galatians, he lists idolatry right alongside the works of the flesh, like sorcery, drunkenness, and orgies, and then warns: \"I warn you, as I warned you before, that those who do such things will not inherit the kingdom of God.\""
    ],
